* Forenoon watch (08:29)  The pilot, Mr Tregenza of Falmouth, came aboard from the cutter to pilot her in (American colours being no bar at Falmouth).
* Forenoon watch (08:29)  The pilot says: Keep the fair way and the lead going; there is a narrow deep channel of sixteen or eighteen fathoms all the way into Carrick Road. The Black Rock lies nearly in the middle of the entrance and shows itself at half tide; the eastern channel is the better, and you may borrow on St Mawes side in five or six fathoms. Killiganoon house on with Mylor Point, north a quarter east, carries you in mid-channel through Carrick Road; St Keverne church open east of Pendennis Point leads into St Just Pool. Moored in the Road, keep the hawse open to the southward, for southerly winds throw in much sea. Carrick Road, between St Mawes and Falmouth banks, seven to seventeen fathoms, the usual anchorage for ships of war; a small vessel may lie off the town in three fathoms, the packets' quay under Pendennis. High water at Falmouth about five o'clock in the evening, the tide rising some 17 feet; the flood will serve from about eleven o'clock in the morning.
  Forenoon watch (08:29)  The pilot's news: Britain at war with the Batavian Republic, France and Spain.
  Forenoon watch (08:29)  Standing orders 'the pilot boards' and 'the pilot aboard' (both the captain's) give contrary orders on the fore topsail; the later stands.
